      "message": "This can\u0027t happen for java APIs, because we only have one set of non-final APIs that become the final APIs.  You can\u0027t add an S java API until after R is finalized (modulo weirdness across branches).  The build system was modeled on the java behavior, which is why \"current\" is not confusing for java.  I guess the behavior is different for NDK because you annotate each API with the version it was introduced in?  It\u0027s probably especially a problem for NDK APIs in AOSP, since the R APIs are not finalized in AOSP until the R release, long after S development has started.\n\nIn the example you gave, what branch are you imagining?  On rvc-dev I wouldn\u0027t expect there to be any S APIs (maybe incorrect for NDK APIs?), and so \"current\" goes from non-final \"R\" to final \"30\" at finalization.  On master, it goes from non-final \"R+S\" to non-final \"S\" at finalization.  In AOSP, it goes from non-final \"partial R + partial S\" to non-final \"partial S\" at R release to AOSP.",
